<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simla Convention</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Simla</a> Convention was a treaty negotiated at Simla in 1913–1914 by representatives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> on the status and borders of Tibet. It proposed dividing Tibet into an autonomous Outer Tibet and an Inner Tibet under closer Chinese control. China's delegate initialled the draft but his government refused to sign, and Britain and Tibet signed it alone, together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mcmahon-line">McMahon Line</a> border agreement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simla-convention.jpg" alt="Simla Convention" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simla_Conference_1913.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> had been a self-governing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a>, but as the Qing weakened, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> feared Russian influence there, carried through the contacts between the Buryat Agvan Dorzhiev and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thubten-gyatso">13th Dalai Lama</a>. British forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-younghusband">Francis Younghusband</a> entered Tibet in 1904 and concluded the Lhasa Convention. In 1906 the Chinese envoy Tang Shaoyi insisted on Chinese sovereignty, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-russian-convention">Anglo-Russian Convention</a> of 1907 Britain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> acknowledged Chinese suzerainty. A Qing expedition of 1910 came close to reconquering Tibet before the dynasty fell in the 1911 Revolution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The conference opened at the Viceregal Lodge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Simla</a> on 13 October 1913 and held eight formal sessions in Simla and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. Sir Henry McMahon, Foreign Secretary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, represented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, assisted by Charles Alfred Bell and Archibald Rose; Ivan Chen represented the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>; and Paljor Dorje Shatra, known as Lonchen Shatra, represented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. Shatra opened by declaring Tibet an independent state and claiming frontiers reaching the Kuenlun Range, while Chen claimed Chinese occupation as far west as Giamda. Shatra backed his case with taxation and administrative records for the regions up to Tachienlu.</p><p>On 17 February 1914 McMahon proposed the division into Inner and Outer Tibet, with a map of both lines. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> repudiated Chen's initialling of 27 April as unauthorised and asked to move the talks to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> refused. At the final session on 3 July, convened at 11:15 pm to give Chen time for instructions that never came, Chen declined, and Britain and Tibet adopted the convention with a bilateral declaration denying China any privileges under it until China accepted it. The border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mcmahon-line">McMahon Line</a>, had been negotiated by the British and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> alone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In 1915 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a> regarded the bipartite treaty as for the present invalid, and in 1921, with the Anglo-Russian Convention held to have lapsed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> felt free to deal with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> directly. Britain sold the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> 5,000 guns and half a million rounds, and in fighting from 1917 they recaptured Chamdo before a truce arranged by the British set the border on the Upper Yangtse. The Tibetologist Melvyn Goldstein judged that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Simla</a> did nothing to resolve the Tibet question. On 29 October 2008 Britain dropped its old position of Chinese suzerainty and recognised Chinese sovereignty over Tibet.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Simla Convention” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the <b>Simla Convention</b> of 1914 Britain and Tibet agreed a border (the McMahon Line), but China refused to ratify the convention.</blockquote><a href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet (1912-1951)</a></li></ul></section>
